{"reference":"Esther 10","book":"Esther","chapter":10,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"Reading the Story’s Style","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"The narrative leading to this conclusion combines detailed Persian court coloring with deliberate stylization, satire, striking reversals, and repeated improbabilities. Readers differ over how best to describe this blend: labels such as history, court tale, comedy, or historical novella each emphasize a feature of the book rather than settling every historical question. Its textual form also matters: Greek Esther expands and interprets the story with dreams, prayers, divine naming, and fuller documents, so Jewish, Protestant, Catholic, and Orthodox readers encounter materially different canonical forms.","verse_refs":["Esther 1:1-22","Esther 2:1-23","Esther 3:1-15","Esther 4:1-17","Esther 5:1-14","Esther 6:1-14","Esther 7:1-10","Esther 8:1-17","Esther 9:1-32"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_esther_010_6deb9c38ff59fc955b987b48"]}]}],"generated_metadata":null}